  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Tonight:\n\nUpper level ridging continues to hold over the region high \npressure lingering over the Ohio River Valley. Farther west over\nthe northern Rockies a surface low has taken shape but will not\nsee much movement until an upper level trough kicks it into \ngear tomorrow. This leaves us with southwesterly flow staying \nsteady allowing for warm air advection and weak isentropic lift \nover the Northland. Satellite shows that this weak lift has \nmanifested stratus that has engulfed most of the Northland with \nsome erosion found over NW WI. Dry air above the moist layer \ncoupled with some weak forcing and lowering of the cloud deck \ncould result in sporadic areas of drizzle. Overall, expect our \nquiet weather and above normal temperatures to continue. \nOvernight low temps were given a slight bump as well given the \nwarm air advection and widespread cloud cover expected. \n\nWednesday/Thursday:\n\nNot a lot of change as we head into tomorrow. An upper level \ntrough moves out of the Pacific Northwest and will help to \npropagate the low pressure over the Northern Rockies into the \nNorthern Plains. In response we will see a tightening pressure \ngradient over the region with slightly strong winds out of the \nsouthwest. Continued cloud cover is expected with some areas of \ndrizzle possible across northern MN. On Thursday the \naforementioned surface low moves through NW MN and into Ontario.\nThis track does not provide much support for rain across the \nBorderlands with probabilities dropping to 30-40%. \n\nFriday-Weekend:\n\nA much deeper trough is set to dig through the Front Range on \nFriday ejecting another low pressure into the the Upper Midwest.\nModel guidance is settling in on a similar storm track as \nThursday's system. However, since the starting point is a bit \nfurther south we should see some increased precipitation chances\ndespite the low largely moving north of the region. The \ndeformation zone will elude the region but the trailing cold \nfront will act as ample forcing for rain to sweep across the \nregion. A secondary low could also form within the frontal \nboundary as we head into Saturday, or at the very least some \nimpressive FGEN banding to promote some better precipitation \nrates. Initial onset precipitation from the parent low pressure \nwill begin Friday with the possible banded precipitation taking \nshape on Saturday. Fairly high confidence in the set up for \nFriday leading to precipitation amounts largely less than 0.25\" \nacross northern MN. Placement of the banded precipitation has \nlower confidence but could provide an additional 0.50-0.75 of \nrain with NW WI currently having the best odds of seeing this \noutcome. \n\nSunday the system largely departs to the east with wrap around \nmoisture still swirling over the Northland. By this time we will\nsee much colder air filtering in from the northwest that would \nsupport the production of snow. Questions still remain on how \nmuch lingering moisture the atmosphere will have to play with \nbut the current forecast suggests accumulations of half an inch \nwill be possible across the Borderlands with some accumulation \nalso possible along the South Shore as lake effect process \nbegins. \n\nNext Week:\n\nA much cooler air mass settles in to start the next work week \nwith highs hovering in the low 30s and the overnight lows in the\nteens.",
